In article <9003130209.AA11719@cie.uoregon.edu> Greg Hudson writes:
>     I thought the interface was intended to be designed to have humans
>make allowances for it.  I'm personally lacking in the patience to spend
>hours at a time on only a few hundred moves, which is the only way I could
>avoid such errors.
I agree completely with Greg.  There is nothing *fun* or realistic
about attacking a moster that you don't intend to attack.  In order to
win nethack it is practically necessary to devote a few hours to
serving your god.  Although I love most of the game, this phase is
exceedingly tedious.  Before long, the dwarves completely trash
the physical layout of the level and then SHIFT-move commands often
go only one square or else all the way across the screen.  It is a very
hard programming problem to fix this behavior, so I do not request it
of the dev team.  However, there should be option settings that allow
ordinary movement without hitting.  Allowing the user to provide (via
the option settings) a list of monsters not to hit and items not to
pick up would also make the game more fun.  The first time you hit
a floating eye or acid blob, it is cute, but it ceases to be funny
after a while.

People like Dave Hairston may like to play nethack first thing in the
morning when they are still very alert, but I prefer to play in the
wee hours of the morning when all my other work is done, at which time
my own eyes begin to float, and I occasionally become transfixed to my
screen.  I want a game of strategy, not a game of dexterity.  The
features that Greg suggested do not affect game balance.  If
implemented via options settings, they do not constitute spoilers.
But they would make a very entertaining game a lot more fun to play.

